"Artists have studied proportions for a very long time. The Roman writer Vitruvius divided the face into three equal parts: chin to nose, nose to eyebrows, and eyebrows to hairline. About 1490 Leonardo da Vinci drew his famous Vitruvian Man and wrote measurements beside it. In 1528 the German artist Albrecht Dürer published four books on human proportion, based partly on measuring two to three hundred people.": "bc5bf2dd9b92",
"Ask a friend to draw a face from memory. Very often, the eyes end up high on the head, with only a thin strip of forehead above them. The nose and mouth are fine, and each part looks right on its own. Yet the whole face looks a little strange, as if the top of the head had been sliced off.": "be92dfd05694",
"Ask a friend to draw a face from memory. Very often, the eyes end up high on the head, with only a thin strip of forehead above them. The nose and mouth are fine, and each part looks right on its own. Yet the whole face looks a little strange, as if the top of the head had been sliced off. Scientists have tested this. In 2014 two researchers asked many people to draw faces and found that the eyes were placed too high again and again. They even found the same slip in pictures of faces printed in well-known research articles. Knowing a lot about faces does not stop it. So where do the eyes go? On an adult head, they sit about halfway between the top of the head and the chin. One university drawing guide adds a few more starting points. The bottom of the nose is about halfway between the eyes and the chin. The mouth is about halfway between the nose and the chin. The ears reach from the eyebrows down to the bottom of the nose. Hair makes this harder to see. It covers the top of the skull and often part of the forehead, so the face seems to start lower than it really does. Hair also adds height on top. When you measure, imagine the shape of the skull under the hair. Artists have studied proportions for a very long time. The Roman writer Vitruvius divided the face into three equal parts: chin to nose, nose to eyebrows, and eyebrows to hairline. About 1490 Leonardo da Vinci drew his famous Vitruvian Man and wrote measurements beside it. In 1528 the German artist Albrecht Dürer published four books on human proportion, based partly on measuring two to three hundred people. But no real face follows the rules exactly. When scientists measured the faces of 153 young adults in North America, the old rules did not describe the average face. A baby's head has a very large skull compared with its small face. Proportions are a tool to help you see. The face in front of you is always the final guide.": "c632c0ca9c8c",
